
// draft
argmax(values: Int[]) -> Option<Int>
	note "the index of the first occurrence of a maximum value in the list, or none when the list is empty"
	# The output is None exactly when the values are empty.
	out == None <-> len(values) == 0
	# A returned index is a position in the values: at least 0 and less than the number of values.
	// reaching for: a binder for the present side of an Option, the way `Ok(i)` binds one side of a
	// Result, so I could write `all Some(i) in out: i >= 0 and i < len(values)`; the notation has
	// no way to name the Int inside `out`
	# No value is greater than the value at the returned index.
	// reaching for: the same Option binder, then the one-element slice at the index, as in `all
	// Some(i) in out: all m in values[i .. i + 1]: none v in values: v > m`; without a name for the
	// Int inside `out`, the value at the returned index can't be reached
	# Every value before the returned index is less than the value at the returned index.
	// reaching for: the same Option binder, then the slice before the index against the one-element
	// slice at it, as in `all Some(i) in out: all m in values[i .. i + 1]: all v in values[0 .. i]:
	// v < m`; without a name for the Int inside `out`, neither slice can be bounded
	// example wanted: values [] (the empty list; the library raises here, and the draft reads that
	// as None)
	// example wanted: one value: the only position is the answer
	// example wanted: values [0, 1, 2, 3, 3, 2, 1, 0]: the maximum occurs twice, and the first
	// occurrence decides it
	// example wanted: every value equal: every position holds the maximum, so the first one decides
	// it
	// example wanted: all values negative, such as [-5, -2, -9]: the maximum is below 0, so an
	// answer that assumes a floor of 0 fails here
	// example wanted: the maximum occurs only at the last position: the scan has to reach the end
	// example wanted: values [68, 61, 84, 72] (the docstring's accuracies): one strict maximum in
	// the middle
